{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 240,
    "dDurationMs": 2860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to brilliant.org for supporting\nPBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3100,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What if every single black hole that formed\nin our universe sparked the big bang of a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8620,
    "dDurationMs": 1720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new universe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10340,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cosmological natural selection proposes exactly\nthis - but even better, it claims to be able"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17500,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to test the hypothesis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24200,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Physicists have been struggling for some time\nto figure out why our universe is so comfy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30320,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Why, for example, are the fundamental constants\n- like the mass of the electron or the strength"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34560,
    "dDurationMs": 3020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the forces - just right for the emergence\nof life?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37580,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tweak them too much and life, stars, galaxies,\nthe universe as we know it wouldn’t exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In recent episodes we explored one possible\nexplanation for this - the anthropic principle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48640,
    "dDurationMs": 2120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the idea of the multiverse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50760,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If there are countless universes with different\nfundamental constants, then it’s not surprising"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55020,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that a few exist with the right numbers for\nlife - and certainly not surprising that we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60060,
    "dDurationMs": 2660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "find ourselves in one of those good ones."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62720,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if you don’t like the anthropic principle\n- and many scientists don’t - then rest"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67980,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "assured, there’s an alternative."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70620,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You only need to accept two things: that our\nuniverse formed inside a black hole, and that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75480,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universes can evolve."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78000,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our universe appears, in some sense, designed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81800,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It has finely tuned parameters that seem deliberately\nset for a particular outcome - life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s another example in nature where\nthe illusion of design has a perfectly natural"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94640,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explanation - and that’s life itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97760,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We now know that the fantastic complexity\nof living organisms is an inevitable consequence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103760,
    "dDurationMs": 2920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of evolution by natural selection."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106680,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Inspired by biological evolution, theoretical\nphysicist Lee Smolin came up with Cosmological"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112880,
    "dDurationMs": 1360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Natural Selection."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114280,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It goes like this: the formation of a black\nhole triggers the formation of a new universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119920,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“on the other side” in a new big bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those daughter universes go on to expand and\nmake their own black holes and hence their own"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128120,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "daughter universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130160,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in their formation the fundamental constants\nof the daughter universes are shifted slightly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135720,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and randomly from their parent - mutations\nare introduced."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140440,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some of those shifts improve the daughter\nuniverse’s ability to form new black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145720,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those universes have an advantage in propagating\ntheir cosmic genetics, and so gradually the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152720,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ensemble of all universes get better and better\nat making black holes, just as biological"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158240,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "organisms with helpful mutations can get better\nat surviving and reproducing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163240,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now by happy chance there’s a correlation\nbetween making lots of black holes and making"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168840,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "life - both require stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172680,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The universe that is better at making stars\nis better at making planetary systems is better"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177880,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at making us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179720,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Seems fair enough."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181040,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But is it any more than a cool story?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183560,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bro?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184640,
    "dDurationMs": 7640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s take this apart to ask two questions:\nis it plausible, and is it testable?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192280,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First up, for any of this to make sense black\nholes need to create universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198280,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is by far the most speculative part."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201080,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact we have no idea, and only very tentative\nreasons to think so."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206640,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea originated with one of Lee Smolin’s\nmentors, Bryce deWitt, who postulated that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212280,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when a black hole collapses, its mass doesn’t\nall end up stuck in the central, infinitely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217320,
    "dDurationMs": 1640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dense singularity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218960,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rather it sort of bounces - but unable to\nexit the event horizon of the black hole,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223800,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it forms a new region of spacetime, effectively\ncreating a new universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The details of how this happens is presumably\nburied in the as-yet-unknown theory of quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233320,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234520,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are various proposals for how such a\nbounce might happen - all of which are massively"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238680,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speculative, and perhaps we’ll cover another\ntime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242880,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "John Archibald Wheeler expanded on the procreating\nblack hole idea by suggesting that the fundamental"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constants of these new universes could be different\nto their parents."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This seems plausible - if fundamental constants\ncan change at all then surely it’s in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260440,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "highest possible energy environments, which\nis exactly the end of a black hole collapse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267240,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps the configuration of the geometry\nstring theory’s extra dimensions gets shifted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271880,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- this would do the job."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274960,
    "dDurationMs": 7960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Inspired by this idea, Smolin added one thing:\nwhat if, when universes reproduces, the constants"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282920,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "aren’t randomly reconfigured but rather\nchange only slightly - analogous to a small"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288480,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "number of genetic mutations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290760,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If that were the case, a sort of evolution\nby natural selection would become as inevitable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296320,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as biological evolution."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298360,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have no good reason to believe any of this\nprocreating universe stuff - and Lee Smolin"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304000,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has readily admitted that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306240,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The point is to instead ask: what if it’s\ntrue?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310120,
    "dDurationMs": 2200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What are the consequences?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312320,
    "dDurationMs": 1640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And can we test them?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314000,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The exponential nature of the proposed process\nmeans that the ensemble of all universes should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very quickly be dominated by ones that are\nextremely good and making black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Any given universe may not be totally optimal\nbecause its constants varied randomly from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330320,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its parent - in the same way that any given\nliving organism isn’t the paragon of its"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335440,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kind."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So there’s a prediction: the fundamental\nconstants that define black hole production"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341760,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should be close to optimal in a given universe,\nat least for a given mechanism for making"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348080,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349280,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In our modern universe, black holes are made\nwhen the most massive stars explode as supernovae."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356080,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are other ways to make black holes,\nand we’ll come back to them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359360,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we should expect our universe to be optimized\nfor producing as many of the most massive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365160,
    "dDurationMs": 2200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars as possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367360,
    "dDurationMs": 1480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well is it?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s actually very hard to say, but it does\nseem like there’s some fine-tuning there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373880,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stars are formed when giant clouds of gas\ncollapse under their own gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in order for that to happen the gas needs\nto cool to just a few degrees above absolute"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384200,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "zero, rather than the typical 200-kelvin temperature\nof the typical interstellar nebula."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390320,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That cooling is extremely slow if the gas\nonly contains the hydrogen and helium produced"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396520,
    "dDurationMs": 1120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the big bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397640,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Heavier elements and molecules allow clouds\nto cool and stars to form much more quickly,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404000,
    "dDurationMs": 920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and of these,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404920,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "carbon monoxide is by far the most important coolant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408640,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In addition, gas needs to be shielded from\nthe heating effect of other stars - and that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414080,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seems to require the presence of tiny particles\nof ice and hydrocarbon dust."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420040,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So without carbon, oxygen, water, and chemistry\nin general, far fewer stars and so far fewer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425880,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes would form - and of course these\nfactors also seem to be essential for life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432840,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what about other sources of black holes?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Theoretical physicist and cosmologist Alexander\nVilenkin proposed that if a universe lasts"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442160,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forever then in the distant future, quantum\nfluctuations of that near vacuum will cause"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448200,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes to spontaneously appear - and\ngiven infinite time these will eventually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453400,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "outnumber those produced by stars or stellar\nblack holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458160,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If all this is true then the most black holes\nwould be produced by the biggest universes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464560,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- more space means more chances for these\nquantum fluctuations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467920,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That favours lots of dark energy generating\nrapid expansion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472960,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that is definitely not our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lee Smolin has various arguments against this:\nfor example, we don’t know that our physics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482280,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can really be extrapolated to the insanely\nlong timescales required for these quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487920,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fluctuations to happen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489720,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I would also add that even if Vilenkin’s\nargument holds, there are no doubt different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495000,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regions in the landscape of possible fundamental\nconstants where different types of black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500680,
    "dDurationMs": 1720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are optimized."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This would lead to multiple branches of the\ncosmic genetic tree - some of which correspond"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507600,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to producing lots of stellar black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And naturally we’d find ourselves on one\nof those branches because those also happen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515480,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be the ones that favour life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517000,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But, whoopsie, I just invoked the anthropic\nprinciple, which is exactly what were trying"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522800,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to avoid with this whole idea."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525560,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As speculative as all of this is, Smolin claims\nthere’s a concrete test for the idea."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531880,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If cosmological natural selection is true,\nthen the fundamental parameters favouring"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole production should be optimized\ncompletely independently to those that also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542560,
    "dDurationMs": 2120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "favour the appearance of life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544680,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And he suggests there is one such parameter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547400,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But first some background."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When massive stars die, they actually mostly\nproduce neutron stars - planet sized balls"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554920,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of neutrons so dense that they teeter on the edge\nof collapsing into a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560240,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Black holes only form when the neutron stars\nis above a certain mass limit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565640,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now it may be that in the cores of the most\nmassive neutron stars, some particles can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571680,
    "dDurationMs": 2600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "convert into strange quarks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574280,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The resulting material is even denser than\nthe original neutron star, and so brings the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579000,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star closer to collapse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581400,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the lower the mass of the strange quark,\nthe easier it is to convert lighter particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586720,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into strange quarks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588880,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That in turn means less massive neutron stars\nwould be able to collapse into black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594520,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Surely, then, if universes evolve to maximize\nthe number of black holes, then the strange quark"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601760,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass should be optimized to make the cutoff\nbetween neutron stars and black holes as low"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607200,
    "dDurationMs": 1400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608600,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lee Smolin calculates that optimized cutoff\nat around 2 times the mass of the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614120,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, if this universe is optimized for black\nhole production then there should be no neutron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619800,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars more massive than 2 solar masses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623160,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624160,
    "dDurationMs": 9640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, the most massive known neutron star is 2.17\nsolar masses, discovered just this year."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633800,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now perhaps the extra .17 can be factored into the uncertainties of the theory..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638040,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or perhaps this is the falsification we were\nlooking for."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641960,
    "dDurationMs": 2920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We await Smolin’s comments on this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644880,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’d like to add my own objection: cosmological\nnatural selection is meant to explain the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651920,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fine tuning in the fundamental constants,\nwhich appear to be either set by design or by extreme luck."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It tries to avoid the anthropic principle\nby proposing a natural selection that favours"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664320,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole production, and it’s just a happy\ncoincidence that the same factors also favor life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670400,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But then do we really gain anything?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673400,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It just so happens that carbon and oxygen\nare good for both black hole production and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677800,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "organic molecules ... but what if it was,\nI dunno, beryllium and boron that helped stars"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683240,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "form - or other elements that were useless to life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687720,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we causally disconnect the selection process\nfor cosmic reproduction from the emergence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693240,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of life then it seems we still have to invoke\na good lot of good luck?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698480,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Overall, cosmological natural selection is\nan appealing idea because it seeks a natural"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702560,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explanation for fine tuning, and one that\nparallels a known process in nature - biological"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708920,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evolution by natural selection."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711960,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It also seems to give us predictions that\nwe can try to test and falsify."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717120,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And even though this idea is probably not\ntrue, it’s really important to remember"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721960,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that speculative ideas like this are exactly\nhow we probe the edges of science."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No one of them is likely to be true, but they\nhelp us explore the vast space of all possible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "realities - where somewhere is hidden the\ntrue nature of our reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739040,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or, you know, our universe's momma might be a black hole, and we live in an endlessly evolving, proliferating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745240,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746800,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to brilliant.org for supporting\nPBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749640,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you want to understand astrophysics you’re\ngoing to need to have a solid understanding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754240,
    "dDurationMs": 1720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756000,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Brillaint.org has a course on special relativity\nthat includes interactive challenges and problems"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761040,
    "dDurationMs": 1160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to solve."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762200,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A hands-on approach can guide you through\nthinking strategies for challenging subjects"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767520,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like relativity. In this course you’ll\nbegin by understanding Einstein’s postulates"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772800,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the Lorentz transformations, and as your\nknowledge advances you'll learn how faster"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than light travel can break causality and\nyou'll even solve the famous twin paradox."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783320,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To learn more about Brilliant, go to brilliant.org/Spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787840,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey everyone, thanks for watching - your support each week is what makes this show possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793280,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, totally optional, but one way to help out even more is to become a patreon contributor."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799760,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even 2 bucks a month gets you access to our\nhopping discord channel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804280,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks a ton if you’ve already joined us,\nand today an extra special huge thanks to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809000,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Big Bang supporter Craig Stonaha. Craig,\nas a small token of our appreciation we’ve"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815760,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "contacted our friends at the large hadron\ncollider - they’re going to make you a black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819600,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820800,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Please email us with the configuration of\nfundamental constants you’d prefer and we’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825120,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get it right out to you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826800,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Oddly enough it’s the same shipping company\nas our Merch Store, which you can check out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831400,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at pbsspacetime.com"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834440,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Last week we talked about the doomsday argument\n- the unnerving idea that, statistically speaking,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841040,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are aren't likely to be vastly more generations\nof humans ahead of us than there have been in the past."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847920,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There were a lot of counter arguments - perhaps\ngood ones because apparently we're still here."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854400,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many people come up with a similar objection."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856760,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I'll quote Mr Fantastic, who articulated it\nwell: A human born 2 million years ago would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863480,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "come to the conclusion that the end of the\nworld is nigh, and so would a human born 2"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868400,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "million years from now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870320,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we accept the reasoning of the doomsday\nargument, doesn't this just mean that everyone,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874880,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for all of history, would come to the conclusion\nthat we're all going to die sooner rather"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878720,
    "dDurationMs": 1720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than later?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880440,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This point it totally valid - in fact a cro-magnon\nshould reach the same conclusion and predict"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doom long before the 21st century - and obviously they would be wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892280,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the doomsday argument isn't saying that\nevery member of a species who employs this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 897080,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reasoning to predict their species doom is\ngoing to be right."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900520,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It says that the majority will be right - if\nthey predict that there will be a similar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906520,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "number of future generations as past generations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909000,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And by similar, I mean within a factor of\na few."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911960,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So ancient philosophers would have got it\nwrong - and perhaps we'll eventually also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917040,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be wrong ancient philosophers to some very\ndistant future generation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920880,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The point is if any given individual assumes that we're randomly sample from all generations who thought about the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928040,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doomsday argument then chances are they didn't come near the start of their species."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934840,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, the real problem with the doomsday argument\nisn't that users of it in the distant past"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940040,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would be wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941400,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rather, it's that it's not at all clear that\nit's reasonable to count ourselves as \"randomly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945400,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "selected\" from all of the users of the doomsday argument."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950040,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Zahaqiel highlights this trickiness in defining\nreference class with a great example:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956440,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Step 1: Define reference class as \"homo sapiens\nsapiens existing concurrently with the internet\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963360,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Step 2: Observe that the internet has existed for\napproximately 30 years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968360,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Step 3: Assume self-sampling assumption makes\nsense and internet access is bell curved over"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over the duration of its existence or skewed towards\nlate-phase access..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980400,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then the internet's going to cease to exist some\ntime in the next 30 years guys."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985040,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No one highlights another misuse of this idea."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To quote: \"What are the odds that I'm born\nas the prince of France?\", asked the prince"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 993280,
    "dDurationMs": 1720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of France."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995000,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, from the Prince of France's perspective, the answer to that question is a probability of 1."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001880,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From everyone else's perspective, the answer is a probability of 0."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006000,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this really highlights the challenge in identifying our \"reference class\" for this sort of anthropic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1011840,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reasoning. Now, the Prince of France knows that he's the Prince of France, so from his perspective"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017240,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the likelihood that he's the Prince of France\nis indeed 1."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But imagine the Prince of France was raised secretly in a normal French family."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1025560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He doesn't know who he is - all he knows is\nthat someone in the population is the Prince of France."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031000,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you ask him for the probability that he's\nthe Prince of France he should probably say"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035600,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1 in 33 million, or whatever the male population\nof France is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040480,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The point is that you need to take into account prior knowledge when you're defining your reference"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1046200,
    "dDurationMs": 880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "class."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047080,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nick Bostrom has another nice example of misusing\nthe doomsday argument."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1051200,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Adam and Eve really want to ... you know,\nhook up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1054840,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Except they're afraid of God's wrath if Eve\ngets pregnant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058760,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The serpent comes along and explains that\naccording to the doomsday argument the chances"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1064200,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Eve getting pregnant are nearly zero."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, it's incredibly unlikely that Adam\nand Eve are the first two out of billions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073280,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or trillions of future humans - therefore, odds are, they can have all the fun they want without"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1078800,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "risk of spawning an entire species."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1081920,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But an even better doomsday absurdity was\nfrom thatisjustgreat who says \"I was 30 seconds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087800,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in when I realized the video is probably almost\nover\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1091360,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By that logic, this video is only half way\nthrough."
    } ]
  } ]
}
